Tidal is reported to be moving from labelling fully AI-generated music to removing it. TechCrunch and the music industry site Hypebot report that removals begin on 9 December 2026. TechCrunch says the streaming service announced this on Tuesday 24 November. This explainer sets out what has been reported, what Tidal's own pages say, and what listeners and independent musicians can check before then.

*Disclosure: this post was drafted by Claude, an AI assistant made by Anthropic, and checked by a separate AI reviewer against the cited pages for figures, dates and wording. This is not a full human review. Stackbrook did not test Tidal or any AI music tool and has no affiliate relationship with any company mentioned at the time of writing. The header image is AI-generated. This is general information, not legal or financial advice.*

## How sure can you be about this?

The reports draw on posts Tidal and its staff made on X. Stackbrook could not read Tidal's announcement there, because the site refused the automated fetch. The date and the new removal figures below therefore come from the two news reports. The Tidal policy and help pages we did read on 26 November do not mention 9 December. The main policy page still says Tidal will accept AI-generated music that meets its rules, and shows a last update of 19 October. Hypebot refers to an updated AI Policy, so check the live pages before you act.

![Timeline of Tidal's AI music rules from June 2026 to early 2027, separating Tidal's own pages from news reports](/images/site1/tidal-ai-music-ban-december-9-explained-chart.svg)
*Chart created by Stackbrook from the cited pages.*

## What the reports say will change

Both outlets describe the same core steps for fully AI-generated music:

- Tidal starts removing existing tracks on 9 December.
- Distributors, the companies that deliver releases to streaming services, may no longer send in tracks made wholly by AI.
- Submissions whose metadata declares them fully AI-made are rejected.

Hypebot says the rules cover both distributor deliveries and Tidal Upload, the service's direct upload route. It also reports that audio detection backs up the metadata check, and names Ircam Amplify as the detection provider. Hypebot's accuracy figures for that tool come from the provider's own product claims, so this post does not repeat them.

Songs made with AI as an aid are reported to remain eligible. Hypebot gives stem separation (splitting a recording into its parts) and mixing as examples, and says Tidal will not act against AI-generated compositions for now. Tidal's help page also says it takes no action on AI-generated compositions, meaning the written song as distinct from the recording.

## The numbers, and where they disagree

These figures are reported as Tidal's, and Stackbrook has not verified them independently.

| Reported figure | What it refers to |
|---|---|
| About 43% | Share of tracks delivered in the past 90 days that were detected as AI-generated (Hypebot says wholly AI-generated) |
| About 27 million (6.3%) | Tracks on the platform identified as fully AI-generated |
| 96% | Share of those with no plays (Hypebot specifies the past three months) |
| About 29 million | Estimated removals ("more than" in TechCrunch, "up to" in Hypebot) |
| 80,000 to 85,000 a day | Expected daily rejections |

The reports word the finish line differently. TechCrunch says the removals happen by the end of the year, while Hypebot points to the beginning of 2027. Treat the exact finish date as open.

## What Tidal's own pages already say

The policy dated 29 June set out three main measures. The first is a label: the help page says Tidal tags recordings it determines are wholly AI-generated. The second is money: those recordings are not eligible for royalties, and wholly AI-made uploads cannot earn through direct-to-fan sales. The third is removal in narrower cases: Tidal said that from mid-July it would block or remove AI music linked to fraud, and its pages also bar impersonation and deception.

The wording is not identical everywhere. The policy page defines AI-generated music as wholly or substantially generated, while the help page says action is limited to wholly generated recordings for now. Tidal says detection is not yet reliable enough for the substantial category, and that partly AI-made music may stay unlabelled. The June magazine article also gave 15 July as the date the policy goes into effect, whereas the policy page shows 29 June as effective and refers to mid-July for some measures.

Separately from how a track was made, Tidal's content guidelines bar AI content that imitates an artist's voice, likeness or identity without permission, misleads about who made it, or is used for streaming fraud. They say offending content may be labelled, demonetised or removed, and repeat or serious breaches can cost an uploader or distributor the account.

## If you are a listener

The help page describes a setting that switches off AI-labelled recordings. Once off, such tracks will not play and are cleared from personalised recommendations over time. Daily lists should catch up quickly, while album picks can lag by some days, and any AI-labelled tracks still shown there are greyed out and unplayable. If you find an unlabelled track you believe is AI-made, the help page says to open the support form, choose the option for copyright, trademark or AI tagging help, then AI flag review, then report that the track should have been tagged.

## If you release music

These checks are Stackbrook's suggestions, not Tidal's wording:

1. Ask your distributor how it records AI use in release metadata, and confirm each track is described accurately.
2. Keep project files, session recordings and notes that show how a song was made.
3. Check your catalogue on Tidal for AI tags before and after 9 December.

If a recording is wrongly tagged, the help page gives a similar route: in the support form, choose the copyright, trademark or AI tagging option, then AI flag review, then say the track was wrongly tagged. The stated condition is that the artist did not use AI to create the recording. Neither news report describes a separate appeal for the new removals. Tidal's content guidelines say removal appeals can go to userappeals@tidal.com or as a reply to the removal notice, and are reviewed by people. Whether that covers the December clean-up is not stated.

## What we do not know

The pages read for this post do not explain how Tidal handles a disputed detection under the new rules, how often its detection is wrong on its service, or whether removed tracks can be restored. Tidal says it will expand its tag and actions to substantially AI-generated content as detection improves, but the pages read give no date or say whether removal would follow. Small businesses that use AI-made background music should know these reports concern what is hosted on Tidal; they do not by themselves change the terms of any music tool.
